# Popover

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A popover is a small panel that opens next to the control that opened it, shows a little extra content, and closes again. It does not take over the page. A tooltip, a short explanation or a small menu are the usual uses.

Browsers provide the popover as a built-in feature. Home Assistant does not give dashboard authors a card or an option for it. This page explains how the feature works, where Home Assistant uses it for itself, and what to write on a dashboard instead.

This page covers:

- Popover
  - `[popover]` global attribute and the Popover API

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A popover has two parts: the control that opens it, and the panel. The control is a button. The panel is any element that carries the `popover` attribute. The panel is hidden until it opens, and then it appears in front of everything else on the page, so no other element clips or covers it.

A popover is not a dialog. It has no role and no name of its own, it does not make the rest of the page inert, and it does not move or trap focus.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-popover-global-attribute-popover-api"><code>[popover] Global Attribute / Popover API</code></h3>

The `popover` attribute can go on any element. It takes two values that matter:

- `auto` is the default, and the value used when the attribute is empty. The popover closes when someone presses Esc or clicks outside it, and opening one closes any other `auto` popover.
- `manual` does not close on Esc or an outside click, and does not close other popovers. The page must close it.

A `button` points at a popover with `popovertarget` and the id of the panel. An optional `popovertargetaction` set to `show`, `hide` or `toggle` says what the button does. A script can also call `showPopover()`, `hidePopover()` and `togglePopover()`.

```html
<button type="button" popovertarget="reading-note">More about this reading</button>
<div id="reading-note" popover role="region" aria-label="About this reading">
  The sensor reports every 5 minutes.
</div>
```

The author supplies the role and the name, because the attribute adds neither. Style the open panel with the `:popover-open` selector. All current browsers support it.

**Where Home Assistant uses it.** Home Assistant adds `popover="manual"` inside three of its own elements: the toast, the notification manager and the bottom sheet that slides up on a narrow screen. It adds the attribute only when the browser supports it. None of these is something you can configure.

**What the pickers use.** On a wide screen, a picker opens its list in a popover-style panel, and on a narrow screen it opens a bottom sheet. That panel is a Home Assistant element of its own, not the `popover` attribute. [Combobox / autocomplete](combobox-autocomplete.html), [Date and time picker](date-input.html) and [Label picker](label-picker.html) own the pickers. [Action bar / bottom sheet](action-bar-bottom-sheet.html) owns the sheet.

**On a dashboard.** No card or option exposes `popover`, and the [Markdown card](markdown-card.html) removes the `popover` and `popovertarget` attributes and does not allow `button`. The Civica theme sets no variable for it. Use one of these instead:

- Write the extra text on the card, under a plain heading.
- Open a [more-info dialog](more-info-dialog-and-domain-state-controls.html) from a tap action, for detail about one entity.
- Use a [dialog](dialogs.html) when the person must make a decision.

On a static Civica page, native `popover` works in plain HTML.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A popover needs no script to open and close.
- It appears in front of other content, so a clipped container or a stacking order cannot hide it.
- An `auto` popover closes with Esc and an outside click.
- It is not modal, so it can run off a narrow screen.
- It adds no role and no name, and focus does not move into it unless you set `autofocus`.
- A dashboard author cannot add one.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Keep the panel short. Put anything people need to act on in the page itself.
- Never hide an essential instruction, a warning or a live state in a popover.
- Name the button for what opens: “More about this reading”, not “Info”.
- Make one way to close it obvious.
- Do not open a popover when focus arrives or when a value changes.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: give the panel a role and a name, because the attribute adds none.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard: the button opens it, and Esc closes an `auto` popover.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order: place the panel straight after its button in the page source, so the next Tab key press reaches it.
- 3.2.1 On Focus and 3.2.2 On Input: opening happens on a press, never on focus.
- 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus: applies to a tooltip that appears on hover or focus. It must be dismissible, hoverable and persistent.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: check the open panel at 320 CSS pixels, because a popover is not modal and can overflow.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the button is 44 CSS pixels high in the Civica theme (`ha-button-height`).

